@@ -7,2 +7,75 @@ description: "Health export, structured diagnostics, fleet queries and how to ex

+## Bounded idle cache cleanup
+
+When cached agents pass their configured idle TTL, one idle-sweep cleanup job
+per gateway runner claims and releases them one at a time. Waiting agents stay
+in the cache and are checked again before each claim: current turns, unflushed
+transcripts and recently used or replaced entries remain protected.
+
+The job uses the existing bounded housekeeping executor. Each claimed agent's
+memory commit precedes resource release under its owning profile. Native
+shutdown finalizes agents still cached and drains executor writers before closing
+session databases; a stuck writer retains the existing skip-close protection.
+Submission failure or cancellation before a claim leaves agents cached.
+
+No new setting, pool, timer, forced trim or provider call is added. LRU, pressure
+and explicit-boundary eviction remain separate; this is an idle-sweep concurrency
+bound, not a global cleanup limit or an RSS-saving guarantee. Cleanup can take
+longer or wait behind other housekeeping work. Existing pool threads remain until
+normal shutdown. Unexpected worker failure is logged; unclaimed agents stay cached,
+and no successful teardown is implied for the failed claimed agent.
+
+## Local memory snapshot
+
+`hermes gateway memory` prints one JSON snapshot through the existing private
+local control channel. It does not start or restart a gateway, trim memory,
+collect garbage, evict caches, open session databases or call a model. This is
+an on-demand local diagnostic, separate from OTLP export below.
+Normal CLI bootstrap/recovery remains native; this handler adds no lifecycle action.
+
+Use the serving gateway's launch profile (`hermes -p NAME gateway memory` for
+a standalone named-profile gateway). Targeting follows the native selected-home
+control transport; the command does not search other profiles. Check the returned
+`pid`: RSS belongs to the entire answering process, including all profiles it
+serves, rather than the selected profile alone.
+
+`process.rss_kib` is current resident memory in KiB, not a peak. When the existing
+native observation is unavailable, an on-demand psutil sample supplies current
+RSS, including on macOS. Unsupported anonymous RSS and unavailable measurements
+remain `null`, never an asserted zero. The first fallback query can incur import
+cost. Cache/SQLite counters keep their existing read-only semantics; allocation
+tracing is not enabled or requested by this command.
+
+Exit 0 means a valid snapshot (even if RSS is unknown); exit 1 with an stderr
+"unavailable" diagnostic means no usable observation within the existing
+five-second control-query timeout or another transport/response failure. It does
+not prove the gateway stopped. Invalid arguments exit 2. No retry or lifecycle
+action follows a failure.
+
+## Exported service health
+
+### Keep an unused local dashboard cold
+
+The gateway normally warms the dashboard after publishing READY. To leave a
+loopback dashboard unloaded until its first HTTP/dashboard request, set
+`gateway.dashboard_warmup` to `false` with the native config CLI. The default is
+`true`; missing or unrecognized values preserve warmup. Recognized boolean
+tokens follow the gateway parser. The running gateway uses its launch-profile
+configuration, including when it serves several profiles.
+
+Back up the selected profile first and preserve the old value, then run
+`hermes config set gateway.dashboard_warmup false`. Adopt the setting during the
+supported idle lifecycle; this command does not restart or interrupt work.
+Restore the previous value with `config set`, or `hermes config unset
+gateway.dashboard_warmup` if it was absent.
+
+This skips only optional background scheduling. A listener requiring dashboard
+authentication still preloads its auth providers, and an already-loaded
+dashboard stays loaded. First-request mounting, concurrent singleflight,
+lifespan cleanup, native session WebSockets and authorization retain their
+existing paths. No dashboard unload or idle-retirement policy is added.
+The first dashboard request pays the deferred import cost; active-dashboard
+peak RAM need not decrease. Client-owned idle-exit gateways already skip this
+warmup, so this setting adds no saving there.
+
